        "grammar": [
          {
            "sentence": "Mbappé la calificó de mujer despreciable e indigna, mientras la senadora, lejos de disculparse, amenaza ahora con emprender acciones legales contra el jugador.",
            "point": "lejos de + infinitivo",
            "explanation": "\"Lejos de\" plus an infinitive says that instead of doing the expected thing, someone does the opposite. Here, instead of apologising, she threatens to sue. Note also \"calificar a alguien de\" plus a noun, meaning to label someone as something.",
            "why_tricky": "English speakers reach for \"instead of apologising\", which is \"en lugar de disculparse\". \"Lejos de\" is stronger and more literary, and it always takes the infinitive, never the gerund."
          },
          {
            "sentence": "Aunque los clubes y las federaciones repiten campañas contra la discriminación, los insultos siguen llegando desde las gradas y, cada vez más, desde cuentas públicas de políticos.",
            "point": "aunque + indicativo, seguir + gerundio",
            "explanation": "\"Aunque\" takes the indicative when the fact is real and known: the campaigns do exist. \"Seguir\" plus a gerund expresses an action that continues despite something else.",
            "why_tricky": "Learners overuse the subjunctive after \"aunque\". Use the subjunctive only when the concession is hypothetical or not conceded as fact, as in \"aunque repitan campañas\"."
          },
          {
            "sentence": "Queda por ver si el caso tendrá consecuencias en Paraguay o si, como suele ocurrir, la polémica se apagará en cuanto empiece el siguiente partido.",
            "point": "en cuanto + subjuntivo",
            "explanation": "Time conjunctions such as \"en cuanto\", \"cuando\" and \"tan pronto como\" take the subjunctive when they point to a future event. \"Queda por ver\" is a fixed way of saying it remains to be seen.",
            "why_tricky": "The main clause is in the future (\"se apagará\") but the subordinate clause cannot be. Saying \"en cuanto empezará\" is a very common and very audible mistake."
          }
        ],
