            "back": "to make uncomfortable, to unsettle",
            "example": "La pregunta incomodó al portavoz.",
            "tier": "B2"
          },
          {
            "front": "reforzar",
            "pos": "verb",
            "back": "to strengthen, to reinforce",
            "example": "Quieren reforzar la producción local.",
            "tier": "B1"
          },
          {
            "front": "el compromiso",
            "pos": "noun",
            "back": "commitment, pledge",
            "example": "Los compromisos firmados en la cumbre son vinculantes.",
            "tier": "B1"
          },
          {
            "front": "la desconfianza",
            "pos": "noun",
            "back": "mistrust, distrust",
            "example": "La desconfianza entre los socios creció durante el año.",
            "tier": "B2"
          },
          {
            "front": "ganar músculo",
            "pos": "phrase",
            "back": "to gain strength, to bulk up (figurative)",
            "example": "La empresa ganó músculo tras la fusión.",
            "tier": "C1"
          }
        ],
        "grammar": [
          {
            "sentence": "El anuncio llega durante la cumbre de la Alianza, en un momento en que Washington presiona a sus socios para que asuman una parte mayor de la factura.",
            "point": "para que + subjuntivo",
            "explanation": "When the purpose clause has a different subject from the main verb, Spanish requires \"para que\" plus the subjunctive. Washington presses, but the partners are the ones who would shoulder the bill.",
            "why_tricky": "If the subject were the same you would need a plain infinitive: \"Washington presiona para asumir\" would mean Washington itself takes on the cost, which changes the meaning completely."
          },
          {
            "sentence": "Buena parte de ese dinero acabará en empresas estadounidenses, algo que incomoda a los gobiernos que quieren reforzar la industria europea.",
            "point": "\"algo que\" as a summarising relative",
            "explanation": "\"Algo que\" refers back to the whole preceding idea, not to a single noun. It is the standard way to comment on a fact you have just stated. Note too that \"acabar en\" here means to end up somewhere.",
            "why_tricky": "English uses a bare \"which\" for this (\"which annoys governments\"). Spanish cannot use \"lo cual\" or \"que\" alone in this position without sounding odd; \"algo que\" is the natural journalistic choice."
          },
          {
            "sentence": "Si los compromisos se cumplen, la OTAN ganará músculo; si se quedan en titulares, la desconfianza entre las dos orillas del Atlántico seguirá creciendo.",
            "point": "condicional real: si + presente, futuro",
            "explanation": "A real or open condition uses the present indicative after \"si\" and the future in the main clause. \"Se cumplen\" is a passive reflexive: the commitments are honoured.",
            "why_tricky": "Never put the future after \"si\". \"Si los compromisos se cumplirán\" is ungrammatical, though the English \"if they will be honoured\" tempts learners into it."
          }
        ],
