          },
          {
            "front": "consigo misma",
            "pos": "phrase",
            "back": "with herself (reflexive prepositional pronoun)",
            "example": "Hablaba consigo misma para no dormirse.",
            "tier": "B2"
          }
        ],
        "grammar": [
          {
            "sentence": "En una entrevista con la BBC contó que sobrevivió comiendo kétchup y queso, y que hablar consigo misma la ayudó a mantenerse consciente hasta que los rescatistas la encontraron.",
            "point": "gerundio de modo y estilo indirecto",
            "explanation": "The gerund \"comiendo\" answers the question how, so it expresses the means of surviving. Reported speech keeps the preterite here because the whole account is finished and past. \"Consigo\" is the reflexive form of \"con él, con ella\".",
            "why_tricky": "Learners write \"con sí misma\", which does not exist, and often use \"por comer\" for the means, which would suggest cause rather than method."
          },
          {
            "sentence": "Los equipos de rescate trabajan contra el reloj, conscientes de que las posibilidades de encontrar supervivientes se reducen con cada hora que pasa.",
            "point": "pasiva refleja con se",
            "explanation": "\"Se reducen\" presents the shrinking as something that simply happens, with no agent named. Spanish prefers this construction where English uses either the passive or an intransitive verb.",
            "why_tricky": "The verb agrees with \"las posibilidades\", not with an invisible subject, so it is plural. Writing \"se reduce las posibilidades\" is a frequent slip."
          },
          {
            "sentence": "Cuando termine la emergencia, llegará la parte más larga: reconstruir las viviendas y, sobre todo, las vidas de quienes lo perdieron todo.",
            "point": "cuando + subjuntivo para el futuro",
            "explanation": "\"Cuando\" referring to a future moment takes the present subjunctive, while the main clause takes the future. Note \"quienes\" meaning those who, and the redundant \"lo\" in \"lo perdieron todo\", which is obligatory.",
            "why_tricky": "\"Cuando terminará la emergencia\" is only possible as a direct question. As a time clause it must be subjunctive, and the redundant \"lo\" cannot be dropped."
          }
        ],
